---
/* DRAFT-FOR-ED — candidate node page, /next preview namespace. */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import MarkNote from '../../components/MarkNote.astro'

export const prerender = true
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Two university CMS platforms"
  description="Two content platforms co-built from scratch at a university, carried through two migrations."
>
  <article class="node">
    <p class="draft-stamp">draft · under review</p>
    <header data-mark="cms:masthead">
      <h1>Two university CMS platforms</h1>
      <p class="dek">Built from scratch, run for years, replaced deliberately — twice.</p>
      <p class="meta">work · retired after long service · ColdFusion, SharePoint, Drupal</p>
    </header>

    <section data-mark="cms:what">
      <h2>The platforms</h2>
      <p>
        Before off-the-shelf content management was good enough for a university, the
        university built its own. I co-built two of those platforms from scratch at UMass
        Amherst — and then did the harder thing: led the migration from the first onto
        the second, and later from the second onto Drupal, carrying thirteen years of
        institutional content across three generations of systems without losing it.
        Building a platform is one skill. Retiring one you built, on purpose, without
        breaking the institution that depends on it, is another.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="cms:cybersource">
      <h2>The payment integration</h2>
      <p>
        The university needed to take credit-card payments online before that was
        routine. I wrote the first working round-trip to the payment processor —
        request, response, and the error handling for everything between — as an
        individual contributor. The integration became a program; I became its lead,
        with two developers. When I moved on, the program kept running without me,
        on onboarding manuals I wrote so that it could.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="cms:concurrency">
      <h2>All at once</h2>
      <p>
        Through those years the platform work, SharePoint administration for
        departmental sites, and a Liferay portal ran concurrently — three systems, three
        user communities, one calendar. Weekly SharePoint labs taught departments to
        match tools to their actual needs, including one metadata redesign that ended
        Finance's standing quarterly-audit document problem.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <MarkNote />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .node {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-12) var(--page-gutter) var(--space-24);
  }
  .draft-stamp {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-8);
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
  }
  .dek {
    font-size: var(--font-size-md);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
  }
  .meta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-8);
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-4);
  }
  p {
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
